(zane-1-bughunt-log
  :owner "Zane #1"
  :date "2026-07-17"
  :baseline (:tests 324 :pass 324 :fail 0)
  :current  (:tests 396 :pass 396 :fail 0)
  :fixes
  ((:id 1
    :file "lib/math/pedagogy.js"
    :verb "math/round-to-place"
    :severity :high
    :symptom "(math/round-to-place 21.44 -1) → 21.400000000000002 (IEEE-754 ghost)"
    :cause "Naive Math.round(x/p)*p where p=Math.pow(10,-1)=0.1 (inexact) — the divide+multiply reintroduces the ghost even after rounding to the correct integer count."
    :fix "For negative place, shift via exponential notation Math.round(x+'e'+digits) then Number(shifted+'e-'+digits) — the shift happens at parse time (exact) instead of a float divide."
    :tests-added 4)
   (:id 2
    :file "lib/math/pedagogy.js"
    :verb "math/expanded-form"
    :severity :medium
    :symptom "(math/expanded-form 0) → () (empty list)"
    :cause "Zero digits are skipped by `if (d === 0) continue`; for n=0 every digit is 0, so nothing gets pushed."
    :fix "Special-case nn===0 → return [0] before the digit loop."
    :tests-added 2)
   (:id 3
    :file "src/base.js"
    :verb "eq?, equal?"
    :severity :high
    :symptom "(eq? (new Sym \"foo\") (new Sym \"foo\")) → #f; smart-equality behaviour advertised in first def was silently shadowed by strict === redef 180 lines later."
    :cause "eq? and equal? were defined TWICE (lines 116-117 with _eqQ smart equality, then AGAIN at 297-298 with weaker JS === and deepEqual). The second definition won because env.define uses Map.set."
    :fix "Remove the duplicate weak definitions at 297-298; keep the _eqQ-based smart-equality definitions in force. Documented via comment referencing the previously silent shadow."
    :tests-added 2)
   (:id 4
    :file "src/reader.js"
    :verb "tokenize"
    :severity :medium
    :symptom "(define (fx-convert-banker's x) x) tokenizes banker's as [banker, quote, s] → parse succeeds with weird tree → lambda parser complains 'lambda parameters must be identifiers'."
    :cause "Apostrophe (') is a quote reader macro and a DELIM. When it appears mid-identifier the tokenizer silently splits the atom, producing a valid-but-nonsensical (quote s) subform buried in the arg list."
    :fix "Detect apostrophe following a non-delimiter character in the tokenizer and throw ReadError with a clear message ('rename the identifier or use spaces'). Bare 'foo, ''foo, and (list 'a 'b) still parse."
    :tests-added 4
    :responded-to-architect "yes — see inbox message @ 2026-07-17T01:22")
   (:id 5
    :file "lib/base/r7rs-small.js"
    :verb "caar/cdar/cddr, caaar/caadr/cadar/cdaar/cdadr/cddar/cdddr, cadddr/cddddr"
    :severity :high
    :symptom "R7RS §6.4.1 car/cdr chain accessors mostly missing. Only 1/3 of 2-deep (cadr), 1/8 of 3-deep (caddr), and 14/16 of 4-deep were defined. Any Scheme program using cddr/caadr/cadddr/etc. errored with 'unbound symbol'."
    :cause "Prior implementation left comment 'base.js has through cadddr / cddddr; the missing set covers the odd shapes' — but base.js only had car/cdr/cadr/caddr, so 'the missing set' was much larger than believed."
    :fix "Register all 2-deep (3), all 3-deep (7 net after base.js's caddr), and all 4-deep (16) accessors via the existing _c helper. Total added: 3 + 7 + 2 = 12 new bindings."
    :tests-added 3)
   (:id 6
    :file "lib/graphics/framebuffer-verbs.js"
    :verb "after-frame / end-frame"
    :severity :high
    :symptom "(after-frame handler) registered but never fired. Callback registrations accumulated forever with no consumer."
    :cause "end-frame implementation returned the frame counter without walking _trace.afterFrame. The list existed but nothing invoked it."
    :fix "end-frame now drains _trace.afterFrame (one-shot per registration to prevent leaks) and invokes each with the frame number. Individual callback throws are swallowed so one broken handler doesn't brick the sibling handlers."
    :tests-added 2)
   (:id 7
    :file "lib/math/basic.js"
    :verb "math/gcd, math/lcm"
    :severity :medium
    :symptom "(math/gcd 3000000000 6000000000) returned wrong result because operands were bitwise-truncated to 32-bit signed."
    :cause "`a | 0` truncates numbers above 2^31 into negative 32-bit signed ints — a well-known JS pitfall. gcd and lcm both used the bitwise pattern for their int-coercion step."
    :fix "Replace `Math.abs(x | 0)` with `Math.abs(Math.trunc(x))` — preserves the 'truncate to integer part' semantics without the 32-bit ceiling. Works for any IEEE-754 safe integer (up to 2^53)."
    :tests-added 2)
   (:id 8
    :file "src/introspect.js"
    :verb "help()"
    :severity :low
    :symptom "help('newline').arity → null; expected 0. Any zero-arity verb reported no arity."
    :cause "`meta.arity || null` truthy-coerces 0 to null. Zero is a legitimate arity for verbs like newline, pi, rng-uniform."
    :fix "Change || to ?? so only null/undefined become null; 0 passes through."
    :tests-added 1))
  :inbox-responses
  ((:from "Zane #1" :to "architect"
    :at "2026-07-17T01:30"
    :re "Reader/reader.js bug: apostrophe in identifier crashes lambda parse"
    :action :fixed
    :approach "Chose option 2 (clean error at reader time). Rationale: allowing apostrophe in identifiers would require special-casing based on previous char anyway, and the resulting reader-macro ambiguity ('a is quote-a, banker's is banker-'-s) is worse UX than a clear rename hint. Full R7RS |foo's| pipes syntax is heavier than the win. Reader now raises ReadError with the message 'apostrophe (\\') inside identifier — Motoi uses \\' as quote; rename the identifier (banker-s, banker_s) or use spaces'."
    :fix-location "src/reader.js tokenize() — see fixes id 4")))
